## Summary: Code Block Styling in AI Chat Messages

Based on my thorough analysis of the design system, here's how code blocks are currently styled inside AI chat messages:

### Key Components & Files

**Component Files:**
- `/c/Users/stick/Github/dooph/ds-sb-chat-code-well-dark-green-20261011211250/src/components/AIChat/AITextPart.tsx` (lines 1-49)
  - Shell component that renders markdown content with class `ds-chat-prose`
  - No direct code styling; renders whatever children it receives

- `/c/Users/stick/Github/dooph/ds-sb-chat-code-well-dark-green-20261011211250/src/components/AIChat/AIThinkingPart.tsx` (lines 130, 210)
  - Also uses `ds-chat-prose` class for transcript styling

**CSS Stylesheet Files:**

### Current Code Block Styling

**Inline Code** (from `/c/Users/stick/Github/dooph/ds-sb-chat-code-well-dark-green-20261011211250/src/styles/dooph-component-tokens.css`, lines 903-914):
```css
.ds-chat-prose code {
  font-family: var(--ui-font-mono);
  font-optical-sizing: auto;
  font-size: var(--ui-text-mono);
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: var(--ui-weight-mono);
  font-variation-settings: var(--ui-font-var-mono);
  letter-spacing: var(--ui-tracking-mono);
  background-color: var(--ui-color-surface-secondary);
  border-radius: var(--ui-chat-prose-code-radius);
  padding: 1px var(--ui-spacing-xxs);
}
```

**Code Block (Pre)** (lines 915-926):
```css
.ds-chat-prose pre {
  overflow-x: auto;
  border-radius: var(--ui-radius-tight);
  background-color: var(--ui-color-surface-secondary);
  padding: var(--ui-spacing-rg);
  box-shadow: var(--ui-shadow-button);
}
.ds-chat-prose pre code {
  background-color: transparent;
  padding: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
}
```

### Token System for Code Styling

**Light Mode Tokens** (from `/c/Users/stick/Github/dooph/ds-sb-chat-code-well-dark-green-20261011211250/src/styles/tokens.css`):

- **Line 279**: `--ui-chat-prose-code-radius: 4px;` — Border radius for inline code chips
- **Line 612**: `--ui-radius-tight: 12px;` — Border radius for code blocks
- **Line 115**: `--ui-color-surface-secondary: #fcfcfc;` — Background color (light gray)
- **Line 628**: `--ui-shadow-button: 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);` — Subtle shadow on code blocks

**Dark Mode Tokens** (lines 765-892):
- **Line 788**: `.dark { --ui-color-surface-secondary: #242425; }` — Dark background (darker gray)
- **Lines 814-820**: Dark mode shadows are darker:
  - `--ui-shadow-button: 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);` (65% black, vs 10% in light)
  - `--ui-shadow-button-secondary: 0 1px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);`

### How Dark Mode Works

The design system uses `.dark` class on the app root (`<html class="dark">`) to activate dark mode. Lines 849-892 show the "Alias re-declarations" pattern where any token whose resolved value changes in dark is re-declared in the `.dark` block to ensure nested `.dark` regions re-resolve correctly.

Currently, there is **no dark-specific token for code block backgrounds**. Code blocks use `--ui-color-surface-secondary` which automatically switches from `#fcfcfc` (light) to `#242425` (dark).

### Styling Approach Summary

1. **No separate "CodeBlock" component** — the system styles raw `<pre>` and `<code>` elements emitted by markdown renderers (Streamdown, react-markdown, MDX, etc.)
2. **ds-chat-prose selector** — A wrapper class that styles ALL descendant elements with inherited color but explicit background handling
3. **Tokens used**:
   - Background: `--ui-color-surface-secondary` (switches per mode)
   - Shadow: `--ui-shadow-button` (dark mode value at line 814)
   - Border radius: `--ui-radius-tight` (12px) for blocks, `--ui-chat-prose-code-radius` (4px) for inline
   - Typography: `--ui-font-mono`, `--ui-text-mono`, `--ui-weight-mono`, `--ui-tracking-mono`
4. **Dark mode behavior**: Automatic through token re-resolution in `.dark` block — no component-level dark-only CSS needed

